is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF area-type configuration on the R2–R10 link (area 4), not the ospfd daemon itself. Mechanism: OSPF Hellos between R2 and R10 show an Options mismatch (E-bit). R10 advertises area 4 as a stub (E-bit cleared), while R2 treats area 4 as a normal area (E-bit set). OSPF requires Hello Options to match; the E-bit mismatch prevents neighbor adjacency from forming, so R10 loses all backbone reachability beyond area 4. Localization: R2 eth2 (21.1.1.1) toward R10 eth0 (21.1.1.2), OSPF area 4. Evidence: 
- R2 OSPF is running and has neighbors in area 0 (show ip ospf neighbor: Full with 39.1.1.1), disproving an ospfd crash.
- Both system logs record Hello Options mismatch: on r2 “Packet 110.1.4.1 [Hello:RECV]: my options: 2, his options 0”; on r10 “Packet 23.1.1.1 [Hello:RECV]: my options: 0, his options 2”.
- Configs: r10 has “area 4 stub”; r2 lacks “area 4 stub” (only “network 21.0.0.0/8 area 4”).
Root cause: R2 was changed to remove the “area 4 stub” statement, creating an area-type mismatch with R10. Corrective fix: Do not restart ospfd. On R2, under router ospf, add “area 4 stub” (to match R10) or remove the stub on R10 so both sides match; then clear OSPF (e.g., clear ip ospf neighbor/process) to reestablish adjacency. Once aligned, R10 will regain a default route and summaries to the backbone. === END ===